WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:02.600
- I think that's so inspiring.

00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:03.660
I know your whole story,

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:04.830
and I know and everybody else doesn't.

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:06.540
So, talk everybody through it,

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:07.500
let's talk about your stroke,

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:08.660
like when that happened.

00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:10.150
- So I'm legally obligated to explain,

00:00:10.150 --> 00:00:10.983
I'm not a doctor,

00:00:10.983 --> 00:00:12.237
I've just played one on T.V.

00:00:12.237 --> 00:00:13.485
(laughing)

00:00:13.485 --> 00:00:15.390
But my specific stroke happened,

00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:16.870
it's very glamorous.

00:00:16.870 --> 00:00:18.340
I was at a film festival in Florida

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:20.780
at a premier of a movie I'd just done.

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:23.460
And after winning best supporting actor,

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:24.608
thanks, I'ma brag,

00:00:24.608 --> 00:00:25.441
(laughing)
for those.

00:00:25.441 --> 00:00:28.600
Then I was in the men's
room at the Tampa Airport.

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:29.433
And I just suddenly,

00:00:29.433 --> 00:00:31.300
next thing I know I was on the floor.

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:32.376
- Like out of no where?

00:00:32.376 --> 00:00:33.470
- Out of no where.

00:00:33.470 --> 00:00:34.540
I had a bottled water,

00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:36.260
I was tryin' to screw the cap back on.

00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:37.670
Until my left hand wasn't working.

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:39.170
So, I was like, ah, it's weird.

00:00:39.170 --> 00:00:41.300
It is cause I? Ah, that's not.

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:43.780
And then my leg, my left
leg went out from under me.

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:44.613
- That is crazy.

00:00:44.613 --> 00:00:45.446
Like out of no where?

00:00:45.446 --> 00:00:46.380
- Out of no where.

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:47.900
I'd had a really bad
headache the night before,

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:48.740
which I've now learned

00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:50.830
that if you've ever had the
worst headache in your life,

00:00:50.830 --> 00:00:51.750
you go to the emergency room.

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:52.583
- But no one would know that

00:00:52.583 --> 00:00:53.430
'cause people get migraines all the time.

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:55.094
- Yeah, so you never know.

00:00:55.094 --> 00:00:56.400
- Yeah.

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:59.550
- But somehow these gentlemen
in the men's room decided

00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:01.070
I wasn't either drunk or on drugs.

00:01:01.070 --> 00:01:02.630
And either dragged or

00:01:02.630 --> 00:01:04.078
Thank you, thank you guys whoever you are.

00:01:04.078 --> 00:01:05.160
(chuckles)

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:05.993
And they either carried me

00:01:05.993 --> 00:01:07.580
or dragged me out of the bathroom.

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:09.920
Put me in the waiting area

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:11.820
on a bench kind of like
this with no arm here.

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:12.950
So I kept sliding out this

00:01:12.950 --> 00:01:16.320
'cause I was about to get on
a plane to fly back to L.A.

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:17.390
But I knew my flight was over there,

00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:18.707
so I kept tryin' to get
up and walk that way

00:01:18.707 --> 00:01:20.360
and my leg just kept going out.

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:21.193
- Wow.

00:01:21.193 --> 00:01:22.820
- So I've probably fell like
12 times in the airport.

00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:24.650
- That's incredibly scary.

00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:25.660
- It was pretty freaky.
- Just to not know

00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:26.493
what's happening.

00:01:26.493 --> 00:01:28.010
That would scare me to death.

00:01:28.010 --> 00:01:29.530
I don't know how you did that.

00:01:29.530 --> 00:01:32.210
But the most amazing
part was actually you.

00:01:32.210 --> 00:01:34.320
There ended being a role in This Is Us,

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:37.910
that they based off of your
real life experience, right?

00:01:37.910 --> 00:01:38.830
- Yes.
- Yeah.

00:01:38.830 --> 00:01:41.250
- So Dan Fogelman, who's
the creator of that show,

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:44.930
also created another T.V.
show called Galavant.

00:01:44.930 --> 00:01:47.633
And he would tabs on me
and my recovery process.

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:50.117
And texted me one day, and is like,

00:01:50.117 --> 00:01:51.097
"I think I got somethin' ya."

00:01:51.097 --> 00:01:52.067
"I'm thinking about writing a guy"

00:01:52.067 --> 00:01:54.600
"who's deep in recovery from a stroke."

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:55.505
- Yeah.

00:01:55.505 --> 00:01:56.660
- And I was like, "Sign me up man."

00:01:56.660 --> 00:01:58.240
- And this is while you
were going through recovery,

00:01:58.240 --> 00:01:59.073
learning to walk

00:01:59.073 --> 00:02:00.300
and all of that again, right?
- Exactly.

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:01.133
- That's kinda cool

00:02:01.133 --> 00:02:02.660
'cause it was kinda like a
light at the end of the tunnel.

00:02:02.660 --> 00:02:04.430
Like, "Man, okay, I got this project."

00:02:04.430 --> 00:02:07.573
- It was a huge impetus to
learn how to walk again.

00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:10.120
- Wow, I can image that.

00:02:10.120 --> 00:02:11.420
- You know, and a lot of
people think about strokes,

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:13.000
they think of old people.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:13.833
- It can happen to anyone.

00:02:13.833 --> 00:02:14.666
No, no, no, yeah.

00:02:14.666 --> 00:02:15.980
- So I was 48 when it happened.

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:16.813
- Yeah.

00:02:16.813 --> 00:02:17.730
- Best shape in my life.

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:18.890
Like low cholesterol,

00:02:18.890 --> 00:02:20.904
low blood pressure.

00:02:20.904 --> 00:02:22.590
Usually, is one of these
out of the blue things,

00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:24.050
like, you just never know.

00:02:24.050 --> 00:02:24.883
- Yeah.

00:02:24.883 --> 00:02:25.716
So, you've been,

00:02:25.716 --> 00:02:26.970
you've had this craft you're whole life

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:28.460
and you had to kinda relearn to do things.

00:02:28.460 --> 00:02:30.360
What was your first back like?

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:32.240
- Was actually the
technical first day back

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:34.260
was on Psych: The Movie 2.

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:35.093
- Oh yeah.

00:02:35.093 --> 00:02:36.100
- Lassie Come Home.

00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:37.770
- Yeah.
(claps)

00:02:37.770 --> 00:02:38.627
- Don't applaud.

00:02:38.627 --> 00:02:40.184
(laughing)

00:02:40.184 --> 00:02:43.870
I was really just odd

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:44.880
get back to on set

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:46.260
where I've been this thing my entire life

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:47.560
and it's like muscle memory.

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:48.950
And suddenly these muscles are gone.

00:02:48.950 --> 00:02:50.420
- Yeah.

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:51.800
- So I quickly learned that
I couldn't memorize the lines

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:53.400
as quick as I did.

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:55.330
- So was it harder then, going back?

00:02:55.330 --> 00:02:56.840
Or was is kind of fun and exciting

00:02:56.840 --> 00:02:59.760
because it's, you know this so well?

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:01.200
- Yeah.
- Deep in you, innately.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:02.323
- In fact on Psych,

00:03:03.430 --> 00:03:05.220
such an amazing group of people,

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:07.170
my cast and producers.

00:03:07.170 --> 00:03:08.590
They rewrote the movie

00:03:08.590 --> 00:03:10.640
to really mirror much like this

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:12.740
as of what's going on with me physically.

00:03:12.740 --> 00:03:13.573
- Yeah.

00:03:13.573 --> 00:03:16.110
- So Lassie gets, spoiler alert, shock,

00:03:16.110 --> 00:03:18.250
Shawn has a stroke on the operating table.

00:03:18.250 --> 00:03:19.083
- Wow.

00:03:19.083 --> 00:03:20.310
- So the whole is me in recovery.

